+<h1>Khub OpenRD</h1>
+<p><b>Bring people and skills together with patients to turn real needs into practical solutions.</b></p>
+<p>Open-source collaboration for rare disease needs, led by patients, builders, researchers, designers, and clinicians.</p>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/OpenRDHub">Khub-OpenRD</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenRare2026/OpenRare">OpenRare</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Khub-OpenRD/rare-disease-list/issues">Share a need</a></p>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/README.en.md">Community work (access required)</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/knowledge/README.en.md">Community library (access required)</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/knowledge/plan.en.md">Records and operations proposal (access required)</a></p>
+</div>
+
+## Who We Are
+
+The Khub team was founded in 2024 and launched the OpenRD rare disease open-source community in September 2025. Patients and professionals from different disciplines bring together medicine, technology, design, community operations, education, and communication through open collaboration.
+
+We want people to keep building useful projects together. Patients can define their own needs, help set product priorities, test experiences, and participate as co-creators.
+
+| Community members | Followers across channels | Open-source projects | AI learning program members |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 150+ | 13,000+ | 8+ | 400+ |
+
+## What We Believe
+
+| Principle | What it means |
+|---|---|
+| Patients also help create solutions | Patients, families, and clinicians should help define the direction, minimum useful outcome, and acceptance criteria. |
+| Rare disease needs extend beyond treatment | Daily life, education, employment, social connection, information, and long-term self-management also matter. |
+| Open collaboration makes progress possible | Share problems and capabilities, support handovers, and improve work together. |
+| Everyone can contribute | Technical, medical, design, community, communications, education, patient, family, and student perspectives all have a place. |
+
+## Guide
+
+| What would you like to do? | Start here |
+|---|---|
+| Explore rare disease projects | Visit [OpenRare](https://github.com/OpenRare2026/OpenRare). |
+| Share a real need | Use the existing [needs issue tracker](https://github.com/Khub-OpenRD/rare-disease-list/issues). |
+| Join the work | Bring code, data, design, medical research, or communication skills to a project. |
+| Help build the community | Explore documentation, records, operations, and collaboration in the [participation guide](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/getting-started.en.md). |
+| Offer support | Support project incubation, infrastructure, hackathons, learning programs, data governance, or joint research. |
+
+## Community Materials and Working Records
+
+Meeting notes, discussions, decisions, and new references should remain useful to people joining later. We propose keeping discussion in WeChat groups, drafting collaboratively in Feishu, preserving curated versions in GitHub, and presenting them through one searchable documentation website.
+
+| What would you like to find? | Entry point |
+|---|---|
+| Recent meetings, discussions, and materials | [Community library](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/knowledge/README.en.md) |
+| How to prepare and maintain a record | [Record policy](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/knowledge/record-policy.en.md) |
+| Responsibilities and rollout | [Full proposal](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/knowledge/plan.en.md) |
+
+*The community repository exists and is private; all community routes above require access. This profile update is still awaiting merge. Existing needs and project links remain available.*
+
+## Featured Project
+
+### OpenRare
+
+Rare Disease Agent is an open-source genetic analysis system for rare disease diagnosis scenarios. OpenRare makes otherwise hard-to-find clues searchable and supports collaboration and continuous improvement.
+
+[Visit repository](https://github.com/OpenRare2026/OpenRare) · [Contribute ideas](https://github.com/OpenRare2026/OpenRare/issues)
+
+## OpenRD Projects
+
+| Area | Project and purpose | Status in the existing README |
+|---|---|---|
+| AI / genetic analysis | [OpenRare](https://github.com/OpenRare2026/OpenRare): an open-source genetic analysis system for rare disease diagnosis scenarios | Open-source development |
+| Assistive hardware | 3D-printed assistive glove: support everyday gripping for people experiencing hand weakness | Open source; two testing iterations completed |
+| Patient support | FSHD management platform: AI reports, visual health management, and patient data agency | Internal testing with 50 patients |
+| AI platform | Rare disease information search: help patients, families, and clinicians find and understand information | In training |
+| AI diagnosis research | FSHD early screening tool: explore support for recognizing easily overlooked early signs | Release planned |
+| Soft robotics | Pneumatic capsule muscle-assistance tool: explore lightweight assistance | In development |
+| Research | 48-hour rare disease genetics hackathon: AI and bioinformatics collaboration with clinicians and patients | First edition completed; second in preparation |
+| Open data | Rare disease database: patient-contributed, de-identified materials for research collaboration | In development |
+| Capacity building | AI for rare disease learning program: knowledge bases, educational websites, comics, videos, and small tools | Running; 400+ learners |
+
+Browse the [existing organization repositories](https://github.com/orgs/OpenRDHub/repositories) or the [project-to-repository mapping](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/docs/projects.en.md) (private repository; access required). Project descriptions and repository listings serve different purposes; verified relationships are documented in the mapping.
+
+## How It Works
+
+The existing community model starts with a real need and aims to sustain useful work:
+
+1. Patients or frontline clinicians describe a need.
+2. Contributors form a group across development, design, research, fabrication, and communication.
+3. The group builds an initial useful version.
+4. The people with the need validate the outcome.
+5. More patients try it and provide feedback.
+6. Resources or proceeds return to support community operations.
+
+Each project has an actual needs partner who helps define its direction and acceptance criteria. Any specific arrangement for income or resource allocation needs an explicit agreement; this diagram is not a promise of returns.
+
+## Community Contributors
+
+Khub OpenRD welcomes equal, open collaboration across roles. People can help turn needs into outcomes that can be tested, improved, and maintained.
+
+| Role | Contribution |
+|---|---|
+| Product / design | Understand needs and design useful experiences |
+| Clinicians / researchers | Contribute scientific knowledge and clinical context |
+| Community operations | Connect people, maintain shared information, and support continuity |
+| Frontend | Build interfaces and interactions |
+| Backend | Develop reliable services and data flows |
+| Algorithms | Explore data and models for complex problems |
+| Bioinformatics | Connect biological knowledge and information science |
+| Genetic analysis | Contribute genetic interpretation expertise |
+
+We appreciate everyone contributing to the rare disease community.
+
+<img src="https://image.gulu9832.xyz/khubsupport.png" alt="Community supporters, retained from the existing README" />
+
+## Work With Us
+
+| Partnership | Possible contribution |
+|---|---|
+| Resources / funding | Support projects, infrastructure, hackathons, or learning programs |
+| Technology / product | Bring development, design, and product capabilities into open collaboration |
+| Data / research | Collaborate with clinicians and open-data projects across medicine, algorithms, materials, or genetics |
+| Joint projects | Propose and co-organize a rare disease or technology initiative |
+
+## Join Us
+
+People from technical, medical, design, community, communications, education, patient, and family backgrounds are welcome, including those who simply want to share an idea.
+
+[Share a need](https://github.com/Khub-OpenRD/rare-disease-list/issues) · [Follow OpenRDHub](https://github.com/OpenRDHub) · [Explore community participation](https://github.com/OpenRDHub/community/blob/main/README.en.md)
+
+Douyin: @OpenRD罕见病开源社区
+
+The community's open materials support knowledge organization, patient support, research collaboration, and shared tools. They do not replace professional diagnosis or treatment advice.
